The lesser sac, the largest recess of the peritoneal cavity, is surrounded posteriorly by the pancreas and kidneys, anteriorly by the stomach, and laterally by the liver and spleen. It may also extend to a variable extent between the folds of the greater omentum. Because of Page 951the limited communication from the lesser sac to the major cavity via the foramen of Winslow, suppuration in the lesser sac may exist with little or no involvement of the major cavity. Abscesses in the lesser sac lie between the stomach and the pancreas but may spread to the right and lie anterior to the right kidney and inferior to the liver.\
