Primary bacterial peritonitis caused by either S. pneumoniae or group A streptococci is treated best with high-dose penicillin, ceftriaxone, or cefotaxime. If the pneumococcal strain is highly resistant to these drugs, vancomycin is the preferred drug and it may be prudent to include vancomycin until sensitivities of pneumococci are ascertained. Peritonitis suspected of being caused by methicillin-sensitive S. aureus should be treated with a penicillinase-resistant penicillin (e.g., nafcillin) or with a first-generation cephalosporin (e.g., cefazolin); if the strain is methicillin resistant or if the patient is allergic to penicillin, vancomycin, daptomycin, telavancin, ceftaroline, or linezolid can be used. Daptomycin is recommended in patients with previous vancomycin-resistant enterococcal (VRE) infections or a VRE-positive surveillance swab.22 If Pseudomonas aeruginosa is isolated, an antipseudomonal penicillin, ceftazidime, cefepime, ceftolozane-tazobactam (may be active against strains of MDR P. aeruginosa), ceftazidime-avibactam, cefiderocol, aztreonam, a carbapenem (e.g., imipenem, meropenem), or a fluoroquinolone with good antipseudomonal activity (e.g., ciprofloxacin) could be combined with another antipseudomonal antibiotic, although use of aminoglycosides is discouraged because of nephrotoxicity.83 More recently, multidrug resistance (mainly via extended-spectrum \uc0\u946 -lactamase [ESBL] production) has been encountered among strains of gram-negative bacilli isolated in primary peritonitis. Although this appears to occur with greater frequency in nosocomially acquired and health care\'96associated cases, resistance to ceftriaxone was found in 7% of community-acquired primary peritonitis cases.84 In some geographic areas rates have been found to have increased up to 30% and 40% for cephalosporins and fluoroquinolones, respectively.81,85 In this setting, as well as for patients admitted to the intensive care unit, a carbapenem would be the antimicrobial agent of choice.75\
\
In cases in which there is a strong clinical suspicion of primary bacterial peritonitis but all cultures are sterile, as well as in cases of culture-negative neutrocytic ascites, antimicrobial therapy should be continued. Clinical improvement together with a significant decline in the ascitic fluid PMN leukocyte count of greater than 25% should occur after 24\'9648 hours of antimicrobial therapy if the diagnosis is correct.64,65,86 In most instances, a follow-up fluid analysis to document sterility of an initial positive culture and a marked decrease in PMN leukocyte count is not necessary in most patients with SBP. However, a repeat paracentesis should be considered in cases in which an expected clinical response is lacking or an unusual organism is present.22,87 If the PMN leukocyte count in ascitic fluid continues to be elevated, other diagnoses should be considered. Historically, antimicrobial therapy generally was continued for 10\'9614 days if improvement was noted; however, shorter courses of therapy have been shown to be as efficacious.64 The currently recommended duration of antimicrobial Page 955therapy is 5\'967 days, including patients who were transiently bacteremic.22 Transition to oral antimicrobial agents can be considered after 48 hours in those who have shown clinical improvement and assuming there is an available active and effective oral agent.88,89 Longer treatment courses may be warranted and should be considered in the setting of resistant or unusual bacterial pathogens, and when the clinical response is slower than expected or when results of follow-up of ascitic fluid analysis remain significantly abnormal. Following an appropriate course of treatment, patients should be reevaluated for any signs or symptoms of ongoing or relapsed infection in which case a repeat paracentesis would be indicated. The administration of intraperitoneal antimicrobials is not necessary. Hepatic decompensation along with multiorgan failure are frequent occurrences in patients with cirrhosis with bacterial infections. Renal failure, which develops in 30%\'9640% of primary peritonitis cases is the major cause of death. A meta-analysis of four controlled trials found that intravenous albumin infusion along with antibiotics resulted in a significant decrease in the incidence of acute kidney injury (8% vs. 31%), and a reduction in mortality (16% vs. 35%).83 Renal insufficiency occurs in about one-third of patients with primary peritonitis. Patients with ascitic fluid PMN leukocyte cell counts of at least 250 cells/mm3 and in whom primary peritonitis is clinically suspected should receive 1.5 g albumin/kg body weight within 6 hours of detection and 1 g/kg on day 3. Albumin administration is also indicated when the serum creatinine is greater than 1 mg/dL, blood urea nitrogen is above 30 mg/dL, or total bilirubin is greater than 4 mg/dL.90 In patients with cirrhosis who develop primary peritonitis, the use of nonselective \uc0\u946 -blockers has been associated with an increased risk of mortality, rates of hepatorenal syndrome, and acute kidney injury.91,92 Because their deleterious effect correlates directly with mean arterial pressure, they should be discontinued in patients with hypotension or hyponatremia.93\
\
Patients with peritoneal fluid PMN leukocyte cell counts less than 250 cells/mm3 and signs or symptoms of infection should receive antibiotic therapy as detailed earlier for primary peritonitis, while awaiting results of cultures, because symptomatic patients with monomicrobial nonneutrophilic bacterascites variant are prone to progress to primary peritonitis, even though at the time of the paracentesis it is not known whether the cultures will yield bacteria. Because only 15% of asymptomatic patients with monomicrobial nonneutrophilic bacterial ascites progress to primary peritonitis, this group of otherwise asymptomatic patients usually does not need antibiotics, and observation is appropriate. In these asymptomatic patients, the paracentesis should be repeated as soon as the first culture yields bacteria. Antibiotics are initiated only if signs or symptoms of infection develop or if the second paracentesis demonstrates neutrocytic ascites. Most patients with culture-negative neutrocytic peritoneal fluid (PMN count >250 cells/mm3) have primary peritonitis. In those instances, the same empirical antimicrobial therapy should be given.\
\
Prevention\
Because of the common occurrence and high mortality of primary peritonitis in the presence of cirrhosis and ascites, prevention is a desirable strategy. This is particularly true for patients who are awaiting liver transplantation. Patients with a heightened risk of developing primary peritonitis include those with ascitic protein concentrations of less than 1 g/dL, variceal bleeding, or a prior episode of primary peritonitis. In randomized controlled trials, the use of antibiotic prophylaxis has been proven to decrease infection episodes and lower mortality rates in such individuals. Similarly, prophylaxis is recommended in patients with cirrhosis and GI bleeding and in those admitted to the hospital for unrelated reasons but who are known or found to have low ascitic fluid protein concentrations (Table 76.2). Short-term (7 days) inpatient intravenous ceftriaxone should be given to prevent primary peritonitis in hospitalized patients with advanced cirrhosis and GI bleeding. This can be changed to oral double-strength trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX), ciprofloxacin 500 mg every 12 hours, or norfloxacin 400 mg twice daily (not available in the United States) to complete the course once bleeding has ceased and the patient\'92s condition is stable. Because active bleeding is the risk factor for infection, patients who are undergoing sclerotherapy or variceal banding to prevent bleeding do not require antibiotic prophylaxis.94 \
