The updated 2017 SIS guidelines suggest for initial empirical therapy of lower-risk community-acquired IAIs cefotaxime or ceftriaxone plus metronidazole or ertapenem as the preferred agents; alternatives for these patients include ciprofloxacin plus metronidazole or moxifloxacin. For higher-risk community-acquired IAIs, 2017 SIS guidelines suggest piperacillin-tazobactam, imipenem-cilastatin, meropenem, or cefepime plus metronidazole; alternatives include ceftazidime plus metronidazole or aztreonam plus metronidazole plus vancomycin as an option for higher-risk patients with a severe reaction to \uc0\u946 -lactam agents.\
\
For empirical therapy of patients at risk for IAI with ESBL-producing Enterobacterales, the 2017 SIS guidelines suggest a broad-spectrum carbapenem (imipenem or meropenem), or ceftolozane-tazobactam or ceftazidime-avibactam plus metronidazole as alternatives. For an AmpC-producing Enterbacterales, they consider a broad-spectrum carbapenem or ceftazidime-avibactam plus metronidazole as an alternative. Combinations of a carbapenem or ceftazidime-avibactam as an alternative and an aminoglycoside, a polymyxin, and/or tigecycline are suggested for empirical therapy of patients at risk for infection with CRE.4\
\
The 2021 World Society of Emergency Surgery guidelines suggest for patients with no risk for MDR organism piperacillin/tazobactam by continuous infusion, tigecycline, meropenem, or imipenem. For patients with high risk for MDR organism, the WSES guidelines suggest tigecycline or eravacycline (not active against P. aeruginosa) plus piperacillin/tazobactam, or in critically ill patients meropenem or imipenem plus vancomycin or teicoplanin.\
\
The activities of various antimicrobial agents against the usual peritoneal pathogens and the results of clinical trials are discussed in the following sections, with special consideration given to the development of antimicrobial resistance in the past decade.\
\
Chloramphenicol\
Chloramphenicol is generally a bacteriostatic agent. At a concentration of 16 \uc0\u956 g/mL, chloramphenicol has activity against more than 99% of the anaerobic pathogens involved in intraabdominal infections, especially B. fragilis. In addition it has dependable activity against most gram-positive cocci, including the majority of Enterococcus spp. and is active against many Enterobacterales encountered in intraabdominal infections. P. aeruginosa is generally resistant. The availability of equally effective and potentially less toxic (e.g., aplastic anemia) antimicrobial agents to treat anaerobic infections (e.g., metronidazole, \u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase inhibitors, carbapenems) has all but eliminated the need for chloramphenicol, but it may be an option in resource-poor areas of the world.\
\
Clindamycin\
Clindamycin had been reported previously to inhibit more than 95% of the anaerobes, including B. fragilis, at a concentration of 8 \uc0\u956 g/mL, and early clinical trials established the efficacy of clindamycin plus aminoglycoside. About 15% of the strains of Clostridium spp. other than C. perfringens, Peptococcus spp., and rare strains of Fusobacterium spp. have been reported to be resistant to clindamycin, and clindamycin resistance among B. fragilis has become increasingly recognized in most medical centers.240 Clindamycin is active against certain facultative gram-positive cocci, such as S. aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes, but not against enterococci, and it has no activity against Enterobacterales.\
\
Diarrhea is reported to be the most common side effect of clindamycin therapy,241 occurring at an incidence of 2%\'9620%. The severity of the diarrhea varies, but it may be associated with pseudomembranous colitis in half of patients with diarrhea, as reported in one study.242 For these reasons and because agents with more reliable in\'a0vitro antianaerobic activity are available, clindamycin is no longer recommended for use in combination with other antimicrobial agents Page 964in the treatment of intraabdominal infections, except in patients in whom other agent options are either contraindicated or intolerable.\
\
Metronidazole\
Metronidazole is active against strict anaerobes; inhibits most B. fragilis strains, Fusobacterium spp., and Clostridium spp.; and has a unique bactericidal action against B. fragilis and C. perfringens. Resistance among B. fragilis to metronidazole is rare.240 The in\'a0vitro activity of metronidazole is poor; however, against aerobes, microaerophiles, and anaerobes that may become aerotolerant on subculture (i.e., certain anaerobic gram-positive cocci and sporeless gram-positive rods). Because facultative gram-negative bacilli and microaerophilic gram-positive cocci (which are frequent copathogens in polymicrobial anaerobic infection) are resistant to metronidazole, the drug should be used in combination with another agent that is active against these pathogens to compensate for these defects in its spectrum.\
\
In a retrospective study, the relative incidence of C. difficile\'96associated diarrhea and colonization was found to be lower after use of metronidazole than after clindamycin.243\
\
Tetracyclines\
The large number of resistant anaerobes, especially B. fragilis, precludes the use of older tetracyclines (tetracycline, doxycycline, and minocycline). These antibiotics currently have limited if any utility in the treatment of intraabdominal infections.\
\
The newer tetracycline derivatives include eravacycline and tigecycline. Both eravacycline and tigecycline have been approved as monotherapy by the US FDA for treatment of cIAIs, in particular when ESBLs, AmpC, and CRE are involved.244,245 Eravacycline is a halogenated tetracycline and tigecycline is a glycylcycline derived from minocycline.246 Their spectrum of activity includes a broad range of obligate anaerobes and gram-positive and gram-negative aerobic organisms commonly associated with cIAIs, such as B. fragilis (including tetracycline-resistant strains), Enterobacterales, and enterococci (including vancomycin-resistant enterococci), as well as ESBL-producing, AmpC-producing, and CRE, Acinetobacter baumannii, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and methicillin-resistant staphylococci.247 Eravacycline is twofold to fourfold more potent than tigecycline against these susceptible pathogens. Eravacycline and tigecycline are not active against P. aeruginosa, Proteus mirabilis, Morganella spp., or Providencia spp.248 Tigecycline or eravacycline (as monotherapy) are alternative options for the treatment of cIAI CRE infections when \uc0\u946 -lactam agents either not active or unable to be tolerated.237\
\
A pooled analysis of trials involving tigecycline, including one trial cIAI, demonstrated an increased risk of death among patients receiving tigecycline compared with patients receiving comparator antibiotics, which has since led to a black box warning from the FDA and a recommendation against the use of tigecycline in severe infection.249 Subsequent investigations demonstrated that when high-dose tigecycline is prescribed (200 mg intravenously as a single dose followed 100 mg IV every 12 hours) mortality differences between tigecycline and comparator agents may no longer be evident.237 Thus, if tigecycline is prescribed, high dosages should be administered.\
\
\uc0\u946 -Lactams (Penicillins, Cephalosporins, \u946 -Lactamase inhibitors, Carbapenems, and Aztreonam)\
Penicillins\
\
Because penicillin G, ampicillin, amoxicillin, and piperacillin are inactivated by \uc0\u946 -lactamases produced by many of the pathogens causing IAI, they are used in combination with a \u946 -lactamase inhibitor, which improves activity of these drugs against many \u946 -lactamase\'96producing organisms.250 Although ampicillin/sulbactam (a \u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase inhibitor combination) has an FDA-approved indication for treatment of IAI, it is no longer recommended for this indication because of emerging resistance.4,251 Similarly, the 2017 SIS guidelines also suggest amoxicillin/clavulanate (another a \u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase inhibitor combination) not be used for empirical therapy for complicated IAI, although its oral formulation could be used to complete short-course therapy. Piperacillin/tazobactam, a \u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase inhibitor combination, has a spectrum that includes gram-positive and many gram-negative aerobic and anaerobic organisms. Piperacillin/tazobactam is one of the agents recommended by the 2017 SIS guidelines for empirical treatment of more severe community-acquired complicated IAI, because of its broader-spectrum antimicrobial activity against B. fragilis, many Enterbacterales and gram-positive cocci.4 However, it but does not confer significant activity against the chromosome-encoded, inducible, \u946 -lactamase\'96producing strains of Serratia spp., Enterobacter spp., P. aeruginosa, Citrobacter spp., Acinetobacter spp., and Morganella spp. Newer ESBL-producing strains of these aerobic bacilli may not be inhibited by the addition of tazobactam to piperacillin, and there are few proven clinical efficacy data for this combination agent against such ESBL producers in the treatment of high-inoculum infections that may overcome the action supplied by the \u946 -lactamase inhibitor.252,253 Moreover, tazobactam is not effective in\'a0vitro for inhibiting hydrolysis by AmpC \u946 -lactamases, thereby rendering piperacillin-tazobactam a less desirable empirical agent in settings where gram-negative bacilli pathogens harboring either stable (constitutive) or inducible AmpC production capability.254 Piperacillin/tazobactam has been recommended for the use in high-risk patients with community-acquired IAI. However, the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute (CLSI) has recently revised its interpretive criteria by lowering piperacillin/tazobactam minimal inhibitory concentration (MIC) breakpoints for the susceptible Enterobacterales from \u8804 16 \u956 g of piperacillin/mL to \u8804 8 \u956 g/mL and for resistant Enterobacterales from \u8805 128 to \u8805 32 \u956 g/mL. The CLSI replaced the intermediate category having MIC breakpoints of 32 -64 \u956 g/mL with a susceptible-dose dependent (SDD) category (16 \u956 g/mL). CLSI based the SDD category on available pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic (PK/PD) data demonstrating an improved likelihood of appropriate target attainment for piperacillin-tazobactam MICs of 16 \u956 g/mL with extended IV infusion strategies (4 g administered every 6 hours as a 3-hour IV infusion or 4 g administered every 8 hours as a 4-hour infusion). However, the FDA notes that no clinical studies have directly evaluated the efficacy of piperacillin/tazobactam extended infusions in patients with infections caused by Enterobacterales isolates having an MIC of 16 \u956 g/mL.256 Because of a lack of proven disparity in clinical outcomes using piperacillin-tazobactam or comparator agents for the treatment of IAIs, except in those scenarios in which pathogens possessing high-level resistance to this drug is known or predicted to exist, it is still recommended as empirical treatment but is suggested to be reserved primarily for the use in high-risk patients.4\
\
Temocillin is a 6-\uc0\u945 -methoxy derivative of ticarcillin. Temocillin is only now available in the United Kingdom and some European Union countries.257 Interest in temocillin has been renewed as a carbapenem-sparing antibiotic to treat infections due to ESBL-, AmpC-, and KPC-producing Enterobacterales, because the methoxy group attached to the C6 position of the penam nucleus confers stability against ESBLs, KPC carbapenemases and AmpC \u946 -lactamases258; in one study, 91% of the 260 ESBL-, AmpC-, and KPC-producing Enterobacterales were susceptible to temocillin using the urinary tract infection breakpoint (\u8804 32 mg/L), but only 61.8% were susceptible using the systemic infection breakpoint (\u8804 8 mg/L); KPC-producing isolates were the least susceptible.259 Consequently, temocillin concentrations in infected tissues outside the urinary tract may be inadequate to treat many ESBL-, AmpC- or KPC-producing Enterobacterales infections. Temocillin is also reported not to be associated with a higher risk of Clostridioides difficile infection in humans, and temocillin does not promote antibiotic-associated colitis in animal models.260\
\
Cephalosporins\
\
B. fragilis and other Bacteroides spp. are usually resistant to the first-generation cephalosporins (e.g., cefazolin) and second-generation cephalosporins (e.g., cefuroxime), therefore requiring that these early-generation cephalosporins be combined with metronidazole for the treatment of IAIs. However, citing the fact that there is only indirect evidence and a lack of recent clinical trials supporting the use of cefazolin and cefuroxime in combination with metronidazole for the treatment of patients with IAIs, it is suggested, and preferable, to use other regimens for which more recent evidence has been established.4 The cephamycins, cefoxitin and cefotetan are distinctly more active than cephalosporins against Bacteroides spp., but cefoxitin and cefotetan resistance has become a problem at many medical centers.261 Consequently, although cephamycins are also active against many strains of E. coli, Proteus mirabilis, and K. pneumoniae, cefoxitin and cefotetan cannot be recommended for use in treating IAIs.1 The third-generation cephalosporins (cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, and ceftazidime) and fourth-generation cefepime have shown significantly better activity against the Enterobacterales. Page 965In addition, ceftazidime and cefepime have activity against P. aeruginosa. With a few exceptions, the third- and fourth-generation cephalosporins have relatively poor activity against B. fragilis and other Bacteroides spp., and the addition of metronidazole is required in order to ensure antianaerobic activity. However, under selective pressure of antimicrobial therapy with third-generation cephalosporins, resistance has emerged among Enterobacterales and other gram-negative bacilli. A common mechanism for resistance to third- and fourth-generation oxyimino cephalosporins is production of ESBLs. ESBLs are encoded by genes located on mobile genetic elements, such as plasmids, that can be passed on to other pathogens. These plasmids may also carry genes encoding resistance to multiple other antimicrobial drug classes, including aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones, to render organisms multidrug resistant. MDR organisms (i.e., resistant to three or more classes of antimicrobial drugs), such as ESBL-producing, aminoglycoside- and fluoroquinolone-resistant isolates, have increased over time.262 Although initially the carbapenems were consistently the most active agents against ESBL-producing Enterobacterales, carbapenem-resistance has emerged, with or without the coproduction of an ESBL, in these organisms. ESBL rates for the two most common aerobic gram-negative bacilli, E. coli and Klebsiella spp., isolated from IAIs were found to have increased from 1.7% and 3.2% in 2005 to 7.3% and 13.1% in 2012, respectively, in North America,120 with even greater increases occurring in various other parts of the world.114 ESBLs confer resistance to expanded-spectrum cephalosporins with an oxyimino side chain, such as cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, ceftazidime and cefepime, as well as the penicillins, other cephalosporins (except the cephamycins), and the oxyimino-monobactam aztreonam.237 Routine ESBL testing is not performed by many clinical microbiology laboratories; nonsusceptibility to ceftriaxone (i.e., MIC \uc0\u8805 2 mcg/mL) and susceptibility to cefoxitin can be used as a proxy for ESBL production.\
\
Carbapenems are the treatment of choice for serious infections due to ESBL-producing organisms, but under the selective pressure of carbapenem overuse, carbapenem-resistance has emerged. Carbapenem-sparing strategies for the treatment of ESBL infections have included piperacillin-tazobactam and the cephamycins (cefoxitin and cefotetan), but these drugs are currently not recommended by the 2022 IDSA guidelines for the treatment of ESBL-producing Enterobacterales, even if susceptibility is demonstrated in\'a0vitro, because of insufficient clinical outcomes data.237 Although tazobactam is an inhibitor of most ESBLs, tazobactam\'92s efficacy is diminished in the presence of higher bacterial inocula (the so-called inoculum effect), as would be anticipated in cIAI.263,264\
\
Ceftolozane/tazobactam is a combination of ceftolozane, a newly developed antipseudomonal oxyimino-cephalosporin with structural similarity to ceftazidime, and a widely used \uc0\u946 -lactamase inhibitor, tazobactam. Ceftolozane is active against many P. aeruginosa strains, including those that are drug-resistant, not being affected by common mechanisms of resistance to extended-spectrum cephalosporins in P. aeruginosa, such as overexpression of efflux pumps, loss of the outer membrane porin OprD, and chromosomal AmpC \u946 -lactamase. However, as with other cephalosporins, ceftolozane\'92s activity is compromised in bacteria producing ESBLs, derepressed AmpC \u946 -lactamases, and carbapenemases.265 Ceftolozane, similar to other oxyimino-cephalosporins, has limited activity against ESBL-producing Enterobacterales and when combined with tazobactam has similar problems as piperacillin/tazobactam. Because ceftolozane/tazobactam lacks activity against many gram-positive cocci and enterococci and has variable activity against anaerobes, metronidazole should be added to ceftolozane/tazobactam to ensure for anaerobic coverage in complicated IAIs.\
\
Avibactam, a new \uc0\u946 -lactamase inhibitor that is marketed in combination with ceftazidime, blocks ESBLs. However, the 2022 IDSA guidelines for the treatment of ESBL-producing Enterobacterales prefer to reserve ceftazidime-avibactam for the treatment of infections caused by CRE to preserve its activity.237\
\
AmpC \uc0\u946 -lactamases are produced by some Enterobacterales and nonfermenting gram-negative organisms (P. aeruginosa and A. baumannii). AmpC production in Enterobacterales are either low and inducible chromosomal resistance (e.g., Enterobacter cloacae, Klebsiella aerogenes, and Citrobacter freundii), low and noninducible chromosomal (E. coli), high-level constitutive derepressed or hyperinducible, or plasmid-mediated hyperexpression. With stable chromosomal derepression or plasmid-mediated hyperexpression, production of AmpC is constitutive, that is, always active. Increased AmpC enzyme production results in resistance to third-generation cephalosporins, other cephalosporins, including cephamycins, penicillins, and aztreonam. Carbapenems retain activity. Cefepime retains activity at low AmpC levels, but not at high inocula. Its clinical efficacy in AmpC infections is debatable; The 2022 IDSA guidelines for the treatment of AmpC-producing Enterobacterales recommend a carbapenem when the cefepime MIC is greater than 4 \u956 g/mL, as long as the carbapenem susceptibility is demonstrated.266\
\
Cefiderocol is a conjugate of a cephalosporin moiety and a siderophore. The molecule binds to iron and uses active iron transporters to facilitate bacterial cell entry.267 Cefiderocol is effective despite loss of porin channels, overexpression of efflux pumps, and the presence of ESBLs, AmpC, and carbapenemases, including metallo-\uc0\u946 -lactamases in CRE, carbapenem-resistant P. aeruginosa (CRPA), and carbapenem-resistant A. baumannii (CRAB). Cefiderocol has variable activity against anaerobes and is not active against enterococci and other gram-positive cocci.268 If it is used for cIAI, it must be combined with metronidazole. However, cefiderocol is FDA-approved only for treatment of UTI and hospital-acquired/ventilator acquired pneumonias; it has not as yet been approved for complicated IAIs.\
\
\uc0\u946 -Lactamase inhibitors\
\
The older \uc0\u946 -lactamase inhibitors (sulbactam, clavulanic acid, and tazobactam) do not effectively inhibit AmpC or carbapenemases, but newer \u946 -lactamase inhibitors, such as avibactam, relebactam, and vaborbactam, marketed with ceftazidime, imipenem, and meropenem, respectively, are effective at blocking AmpC and some carbapenemases. Despite the increased potency of newer \u946 -lactams (i.e., cefiderocol) and \u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase inhibitor combination agents (i.e., ceftazidime-avibactam, imipenem-cilastatin-relebactam, and meropenem-vaborbactam) against AmpC infections, the 2022 IDSA currently suggests that these agents be preferentially reserved for treating infections caused by organisms exhibiting carbapenem resistance (see later). Although imipenem/relebactam has been approved as a single agent to treat complicated IAI, ceftazidime/avibactam with metronidazole and meropenem-vaborbactam have not been approved yet by the FDA for treatment of complicated IAIs.\
\
Ampicillin-sulbactam is unique \uc0\u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase combination because the sulbactam component (a \u946 -lactamase inhibitor) has excellent bactericidal activity against A. baumannii even in the absence of ampicillin; however, sulbactam resistance has become an increasing problem. Because, as a \u946 -lactam itself, sulbactam is susceptible to degradation by \u946 -lactamases acquired by Acinetobacter. However, avibactam (marketed with ceftazidime) combined with sulbactam restores sulbactam susceptibility against carbapenem-resistant CRAB.269\
\
Carbapenems\
\
The carbapenem antibiotics imipenem, meropenem, and ertapenem, have a broad antimicrobial spectrum,270 with activity against almost all aerobic and anaerobic pathogens, although E. faecium, Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, and methicillin-resistant staphylococci are resistant. The carbapenems are not inactivated by ESBLs or AmpC \uc0\u946 -lactamases. Meropenem is slightly more active than imipenem against gram-negative bacilli and slightly less active against gram-positive cocci. The activity of ertapenem is similar to that of the other carbapenems, except ertapenem is not active against enterococci and P. aeruginosa, thereby relegating its use to lower-risk patients with community-acquired infections.271 Its once-daily dosing (facilitating outpatient intravenous therapy) and possible decreased induction of MDR gram-negative bacilli272 may make it appealing. However, experts have expressed concern that broad use of ertapenem may hasten the appearance of carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae, Pseudomonas, and Acinetobacter spp.1\
\
The carbapenems are approved by the FDA for treatment of complicated IAIs. However, with their increased use, resistance to carbapenems has developed worldwide. Resistance mechanisms include production of a variety of carbapenemases: KPCs (K. pneumoniae carbapenemases), OXA-48, and metallo-\uc0\u946 -lactamases, such as NDM, VIM, and imipenemases (IMPs). Other carbapenem Page 966resistance mechanisms include increased expression of antibiotic efflux pumps, decreased outer membrane permeability due to reduced expression of transmembrane porin channels, and increased AmpC \u946 -lactamase activity.273 In the United States, about 30% of CRE produce a carbapenemase, usually a KPC; only about 10% of carbapenemases identified in the United States have been metallo-\u946 -lactamases.274 Carbapenemases are also produced by P. aeruginosa, A. baumannii, and other gram-negative bacteria.\
\
The older \uc0\u946 -lactamase inhibitors (clavulanic acid, sulbactam, and tazobactam) do not block KPCs.275 OXA-48 or the metallo-\u946 -lactamases. The newer \u946 -lactamase inhibitors, avibactam (marketed with ceftazidime) and relebactam (marketed with imipenem) that have a diazabicyclooctane core, and vaborbactam (marketed with meropenem) that has a boronic acid core, block the KPCs. Avibactam also blocks OXA-48, but neither vaborbactam nor relebactam block OXA-48 and none of these new \u946 -lactamase inhibitors block the metallo-\u946 -lactamases.276\
\
Aztreonam\
\
An aztreonam-based regimen is a useful option in those patients with known serious allergic reactions to other \uc0\u946 -lactam antibiotics, because cross-reactivity in \u946 -lactam\'96hypersensitive individuals is not known to occur with this monobactam agent; but, to treat IAIs aztreonam requires supplementation by another drug active against anaerobes, such as metronidazole, and another drug active against aerobic gram-positive cocci.277 Aztreonam is active against metallo-\u946 -lactamase\'96producing gram-negative bacilli. To treat complicated IAI caused by suspected or confirmed carbapenem-resistant gram-negative bacilli due to a metallo-\u946 -lactamase, aztreonam can be used in combination with avibactam (currently marketed with ceftazidime), which blocks \u946 -lactamases (ESBLs, KPCs, and AmpC) that would otherwise inactivate aztreonam.266,278 The combination of avibactam and aztreonam is currently under clinical development.279\
\
Aminoglycosides\
\
Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, kanamycin, amikacin, and neomycin) are active against many gram-negative aerobic bacilli and were once considered a major component of intraabdominal infection treatment regimens. Over the decades of aminoglycoside use, aminoglycoside resistance has increased, however, and other antibiotic classes with predictable activity against gram-negative aerobic bacilli have become available. For optimal PD effects of aminoglycoside antibiotics, serum levels should be at least 10 times the MIC of the pathogen. Persistent inhibition of growth of the pathogen after brief exposure to an aminoglycoside (the postantibiotic effect) allows the serum levels of aminoglycosides to decline to less than the MIC of the pathogen during the dosing interval without loss of efficacy. Because aminoglycoside nephrotoxicity and perhaps ototoxicity are dependent on time, not concentration, high peak serum levels to maximize efficacy can be achieved without excess toxicity if the dosing interval is lengthened. This is the rationale for giving the total daily aminoglycoside dose as a single dose every 24 hours in patients with normal renal function, rather than giving the daily dose in two (amikacin) or three (gentamicin or tobramycin) equally divided doses every 24 hours. The numbers of severely ill patients with intraabdominal sepsis studied are too limited, however, to permit recommendation of the general use of single daily dosing of these drugs.\
\
Aminoglycosides are inactive against obligate anaerobes, and their activity against sensitive pathogens is antagonized by an anaerobic environment and by reducing substances, such as sulfhydryl compounds. Aminoglycosides are also not active in acidic conditions. The \uc0\u946 -lactams are probably more reliable antibiotics than the aminoglycosides against sensitive pathogens. In two meta-analyses of well-conducted trials, aminoglycoside-based regimens were deemed inferior compared with several other regimens for the treatment of patients with intraabdominal infections.280,281 For efficacy reasons, and because of toxicity concerns, the use of aminoglycosides to treat intraabdominal infection has been questioned; aminoglycosides are no longer recommended for routine use.1,4 If indicated on the basis of in\'a0vitro sensitivity testing, \u946 -lactams should be used in preference to aminoglycosides. An aminoglycoside may be included with a \u946 -lactam antibiotic in the initial antimicrobial regimen for patients who are critically ill or in whom a resistant pathogen (e.g., P. aeruginosa) is suspected, as in empirical treatment of complicated health care\'96associated infections. They may also be necessary in the severely \u946 -lactam\'96allergic patient infected with a MDR aerobic gram-negative bacilli when no alternative active antibiotic exists.\
\
Plazomicin is an FDA-approved synthetic aminoglycoside for complicated UTI but has also been used to treat CRE infections, including infections due to isolates that produce aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes that reduce susceptibility to other aminoglycosides.282 Plazomicin lacks activity against Enterococcus spp., Streptococcus spp., S. maltophilia, and obligate anaerobes. Plazomicin does not display improved activity over traditional aminoglycosides against other problematic resistant gram-negative bacteria, namely P. aeruginosa and A. baumannii.283,284 Plazomicin has been reportedly fast-tracked for treatment of cIAI.\
\
Fluoroquinolones\
\
The fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, and moxifloxacin) are active against all aerobic gram-negative bacilli. Ciprofloxacin remains the most potent fluoroquinolone against P. aeruginosa. Levofloxacin and moxifloxacin are more active than the older fluoroquinolones against gram-positive cocci, although enterococci and methicillin-resistant S. aureus tend to be less susceptible than other gram-positive cocci. In contrast to other fluoroquinolones, moxifloxacin is active against obligate anaerobes.285,286 Concerns about resistance of anaerobes to moxifloxacin have been raised, although no existing data demonstrate suboptimal clinical outcomes or lower anaerobic susceptibility to this fluoroquinolone.3,287\
\
Fluoroquinolones should be used judiciously; especially because E. coli resistance has emerged internationally.94,288 Fluoroquinolones have now been found to have plasmid-mediated resistance. In treating patients with community- or health care\'96acquired intraabdominal infections of high severity, fluoroquinolone antibiotics should not be used unless local and hospital microbiologic surveys indicate that more than 90% of E. coli strains remain susceptible to these agents.1,3\
\
Currently, three fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, and moxifloxacin) are available for parenteral administration. These fluoroquinolones are also well absorbed after oral administration. If the use of ciprofloxacin was considered for secondary IAIs, the addition of an antimicrobial agent active against anaerobic bacteria and aerobic or microaerophilic gram-positive cocci would be required. The use of levofloxacin, even combined with a second agent with reliable activity against anaerobic gram-negative bacilli for the treatment of IAIs, does not have any published support, and perhaps this fluoroquinolone should be reserved for respiratory and urinary tract infections for which it may be better suited289 or in situations in which a fluoroquinolone is warranted, and it is the only one available for use.4 Moxifloxacin is the only fluoroquinolone that has been studied and approved as a single agent for the treatment of complicated IAIs. It has been determined to be noninferior to \uc0\u946 -lactam/\u946 -lactamase inhibitors, cephalosporin-based regimens, and carbapenems in several comparator studies.290\'96294 However, its use should be cautioned against in patients who have recently been treated with any fluoroquinolone antibiotic because these individuals may harbor moxifloxacin-resistant strains of B. fragilis and other fluoroquinolone-resistant microorganisms. Fluoroquinolone-based IAI regimens are practical treatment options in lower-risk patients who are severely allergic to \u946 -lactam antibiotics in settings where the degree of E. coli fluoroquinolone resistance is not high.\
\
Polymyxins\
Polymyxin B and colistin are last-resort antibiotics to treat infections caused by MDR gram-negative bacteria, when most or all other antimicrobial options are exhausted.295 However, gram-negative bacteria can develop resistance to polymyxins through modifications of the lipopolysaccharide (LPS) structure that inhibit the binding of polymyxins to LPS. Recently the plasmid-encoded mcr gene, which confers the antibiotic resistance, has been isolated from Enterobacterales, A. baumannii, and P. aeruginosa and has spread worldwide. Polymyxins have a relatively narrow antibacterial spectrum, acting against many Enterobacterales, A. baumannii, P. aeruginosa, and S. maltophilia. However, some Enterbacterales, such as Proteus spp., Morganella morganii, Providencia spp., and Serratia marcescens, gram-positive bacteria, and anaerobic bacteria are resistant.\
\
The 2017 SIS guidelines suggest a polymyxin in combination with other antibiotics could be used in patients (e.g., health care\'96acquired Page 967IAI) with confirmed or suspected CRE, drug-resistant P. aeruginosa, and drug-resistant A. baumannii infections, at times in combinations with an aminoglycoside or tigecycline (not applicable for P. aeruginosa). However, the major adverse effects of the polymyxins related to its parenteral use are neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity, with a narrow therapeutic window, and worsened by coadministration of other drugs that are also nephrotoxic (anti-inflammatory drugs, vancomycin, or aminoglycoside antibiotics). The 2022 IDSA guidelines suggest polymyxin B and colistin should not be used for the treatment of infections caused by CRE and do not recommend combination therapy for these infections when susceptibility to a preferred \uc0\u946 -lactam agent has been demonstrated. Listed in Table 76.6 are the possible applications of new antimicrobial agents against gram-negative bacteria based on resistance mechanisms.\
\
Pharmacokinetic and Pharmacodynamic Considerations\
For agents whose pharmacodynamic (PD) killing is time-dependent (e.g., \uc0\u946 -lactams), the PD target is based on the percentage of the dosing interval during which free drug concentration remains above the MIC (free T [time] > MIC), the goal being 40% or more of the dosing interval.296 Historically, this target has been achieved by choosing the dose and dosing interval of antibiotic to be administered based on its pharmacokinetic (PK) properties along with the specific or suspected bacterial pathogen(s) involved as well as the known or presumed site of infection. More recently, continuous or prolonged antibiotic dosing of \u946 -lactams over 3\'964 hours is being used (in critically ill patients) based on the concept that such dosing could optimize the PK/PD features of the agent used with the anticipation that this would enhance clinical efficacy.297 Although prolonged antibiotic infusion has been determined to be nonharmful, a review of individual clinical trials as well as meta-analyses comparing it with standard intermittent infusion has not conclusively found it to be more clinically beneficial.297\'96300 Current guidelines suggest that prolonged infusion of \u946 -lactams is not routinely needed for treatment of patients with IAIs, but is an option for management of IAIs in critically ill patients and those at risk for infections with resistant gram-negative pathogens.4 The antimicrobial effect of antibiotics other than \u946 -lactams (e.g., fluoroquinolones, aminoglycosides, and metronidazole) is based on the PD principle known as concentration-dependent killing, which is directly related to the ratio of the area under the concentration-time curve to the MIC (AUC/MIC) for each agent.301,302\
\
Customarily, recommended doses of antimicrobial agents are used in the treatment of IAIs.303,304 Although initial doses should remain the same, dosage adjustments of renally cleared agents are indicated in patients with impaired kidney function with subsequent dose adjustments made based on the estimated glomerular filtration rate.305 However, the volume of distribution (Vd), as well as the clearance of some antimicrobial agents, is increased in critically ill patients as a result of early goal-directed therapy and volume resuscitation and the subsequent expansion of the extracellular space.306 In addition, critically ill patients may also experience an increase in renal clearance of medications, which together with a larger Vd can lead to suboptimal concentrations of needed antimicrobial agents and ultimately poorer clinical outcomes.307 Under these circumstances, higher doses of antimicrobial agents should be considered.308 Adjustments in dosage of antimicrobial agents that are cleared by hepatic metabolism may also be necessary, because they may accumulate to potentially toxic levels in patients with known or newly acquired hepatic dysfunction.309\
\
Duration of antimicrobial therapy\
A recent study involving adults with community-acquired localized peritonitis observed that patients treated for 3 days had similar outcomes to those treated for 5\'9610 days.310 In another study involving cIAI, patients who had undergone an adequate source-control procedure had similar outcomes after being treated with a fixed-duration antibiotic course of approximately 4 days compared with those after a longer course of antibiotics of approximately 8 days.311 Patients with secondary bacteremia from an abdominal source who received shorter courses of antibiotics, defined as 7 or fewer days, have been shown to have similar outcomes to those given traditional longer courses of antiinfective therapy.312 Only a short course of antimicrobial therapy (24 hours) is required for sterile peritonitis that occurs in the peritoneal space around an infected intraabdominal organ that can be resected, such as the appendix in nonperforated appendicitis, or the gallbladder in acute cholecystitis.313\'96315 Rates of infection in patients receiving perioperative antibiotics alone compared with longer courses of treatment in cases of nonperforated or ischemic GI diseases have proven the same.316 Similarly, contamination of the peritoneum with bacteria from a defect in the intestinal wall (e.g., immediately after penetrating abdominal trauma) also may necessitate only (1) operative intervention to remove the diseased organ and stop continued peritoneal contamination and (2) a brief course of antimicrobial therapy, as would be indicated for surgical prophylaxis, limiting antibiotic administration to no more than 24 hours.317\'96319 Persistent sepsis is suggestive of several complications: formation of an intraabdominal abscess, which necessitates drainage; continued contamination of the peritoneum from an inadequately controlled source; superimposed nosocomial infection with a resistant pathogen; or tertiary peritonitis.\
\
The utility and proven benefit of antibiotic therapy to decrease secondary infection in noninfectious intraabdominal inflammatory disease has been an area of controversy. Quite often antibiotics are given to patients diagnosed with acute necrotizing pancreatitis who do not have a secondary infection complication. However, meta-analyses have failed to demonstrate any significant proven benefit, and the risks of bacterial resistance and complications, such as C. difficile infection, are increased when antibiotics are administered in this prophylactic fashion.320\'96322 Studies looking at the need for antibiotics as part of the management of acute, uncomplicated sigmoid diverticulitis have found no difference in outcomes in those patients who were treated conservatively without antibiotics.323,324 However, it may be prudent to apply this strategy only in lower-risk patients diagnosed with acute, uncomplicated left-sided diverticulitis.325,326 Similarly, a 5-year follow-up of antibiotic therapy alone for uncomplicated appendicitis had similar outcomes as in patients who underwent an appendectomy; the likelihood of late recurrence within 5 years was 39.1%.327 (See Chapter 78.)\
\
Patients able to tolerate oral intake may switch to oral therapy after an initial response to intravenous therapy1,4,328,329; the purpose of the switch should be the intention to shorten the length of parenteral antibiotic administration.330,331 In addition, oral therapy allows earlier discharge for some patients and reduces costs and risks of infusion-related complications. When ileus no longer precludes oral intake, efficacy of oral therapy depends on the adequacy of absorption after oral administration of the antimicrobial agents, the availability of potent oral agents against the significant aerobic and anaerobic gram-negative bacilli and gram-positive cocci pathogens (i.e., E. coli, B. fragilis, and streptococcal species), and the effects of oral versus intravenous administration of these agents on bowel microbiota. The potential oral agents most studied for this purpose include amoxicillin-clavulanate, ciprofloxacin with metronidazole, and moxifloxacin, with recognition of the heightened prevalence of E. coli resistance to these agents. Levofloxacin is often substituted as the fluoroquinolone component but has never been studied for this purpose. Likewise, the oral cephalosporin drugs and TMP-SMX have little proven evidence as antibiotics for the treatment of intraabdominal infections but may be potential treatment options in specific cases.\
\
The addition of antibiotics or antiseptics to intraperitoneal lavage fluid continues to be debated, and its efficacy intraoperatively or by continuous peritoneal lavage postoperatively remains unclear.332,333 One study showed that irrigation of the peritoneal cavity with povidone-iodine decreased the frequency of intraabdominal abscess formation, in comparison with saline irrigation,334 and other studies have suggested that including antibiotics in lavage fluid can reduce the incidence of postoperative infections. Povidone-iodine has been shown to be a potent inactivator of neutrophil functions such as chemotaxis and phagocytosis, however, and may have a detrimental effect.\
\
Gastrointestinal Drainage\
In the presence of peritonitis, the patient should receive nothing by mouth. If no distention is present when treatment is instituted, continuous gastric suction is usually sufficient. For patients with distention when treatment is started and for patients who develop distention despite gastric drainage, the small intestine should be intubated.Page 968\
\
Water and Electrolyte Administration\
The type of fluid replacement is determined in large part by the chemical abnormalities found. Early goal-directed volume resuscitation, previously deemed essential in the treatment of severe sepsis and septic shock,335 has since come under question, because several more recent randomized controlled trials (e.g., ProCESS, ARISE) have questioned this protocol having demonstrated that early goal-directed volume resuscitation for patients presenting to the emergency department with early septic shock did not reduce mortality compared with usual care.336,337 However, instead of standard protocol-driven resuscitation mandates, the measures taken are best chosen based on each patient\'92s clinical end points. Regarding the types of fluids, timing, and amounts to be given, the recommendations put forth in the Surviving Sepsis Campaign are a reasonable starting point to be followed.195\
\
Respiratory Support\
Fluid sequestered in the abdomen and loops of bowel distended by gas may elevate the diaphragm. Inflammation of the parietal peritoneum, including the diaphragmatic surface, leads to guarding and splinting of the muscular wall, which interferes with deep breathing and coughing. A subphrenic abscess may be responsible for splinting of the diaphragm. Arterial blood gas studies are necessary to detect and quantitate respiratory decompensation. Measures aimed primarily at GI decompression, elevation of the head of the bed, and control of the inflammation may improve respiration sufficiently. Administration of oxygen may improve arterial oxygen saturation. If these measures are inadequate, endotracheal intubation or tracheostomy should be performed without delay.\
\
Source Control: Operative Approach and Nonsurgical Intervention\
Surgical intervention is aimed at controlling the most established source of infection and peritoneal contamination and at d\'e9bridement of necrotic tissue and foreign intraperitoneal matter.338 Failure to do so has clearly been demonstrated to be one of the key factors associated with unfavorable outcomes and increased mortality.339,340 Immediate surgical intervention within hours of presentation even in the medically unstable septic patient has been found to have a significant survival advantage.341 In the majority of cases, intervention to control the source, and to restore normal GI function and anatomy should be carried out within 24 hours; studies have indicated that postponement of source control beyond this time period has been associated with a greater risk of death.342 Optimal management includes (1) bowel decompression (e.g., through proximal colostomy for perforation, diverticulitis, or colonic carcinoma); (2) closure of traumatic perforations and resection of a diseased, perforated viscus to stop continued peritoneal contamination with bacteria and adjuvants; and (3) drainage of any purulent collections, which reduces the bacterial inoculum, removes excessive levels of proinflammatory cytokines and adjuvants (e.g., fecal matter, food, blood, bile, barium) that would enhance the virulence of peritoneal infection, and eliminates anaerobic conditions. In the absence of perforation, when the disease process (e.g., acute appendicitis, necrotic bowel) is anticipated to advance, the involved organ is resected. Acute increase in intraabdominal pressure, which itself can lead to multiorgan dysfunction (e.g., acute pulmonary and renal failure, shock), necessitates decompression laparotomy; if necessary, the abdomen is left open and covered with a protective dressing.112\
\
In complicated cases or severely ill patients, it may be necessary to perform an initial laparotomy for immediate source control with temporary abdominal closure followed later by a reexploration for definitive treatment.343,344 Operative intervention is generally not indicated for patients with primary peritonitis; in patients in whom the disease process subsides and localizes while the patient is being prepared for surgery; in moribund patients; or in patients with pelvic peritonitis caused by pelvic inflammatory disease, which usually responds to medical management. Stable, noncritical patients with localized intraabdominal infections, including perforated appendicitis with localized phlegmon or periappendiceal abscesses; acute colonic diverticulitis with inflammation into the adjacent colonic tissues or small abscesses; and localized upper GI perforations, may be treated conservatively with antibiotics alone, at times.345\'96347 A similar treatment approach followed by a delay before surgery of days or even weeks may be warranted in other scenarios.348,349\
\
Intraoperative peritoneal lavage with saline, after drainage of purulent peritoneal exudates, fecal matter, food, and other foreign debris, is standard procedure during laparotomy for peritonitis. Continuous postoperative peritoneal lavage for 48\'9672 hours or until the fluid is clear, with the use of large volumes of fluid to ensure dispersion of the fluid and prevent loculations, has, however, not been effective.332,333 Earlier studies advocating for the inclusion of antibiotics in fluids used for lavage citing reduced postoperative infection complication rates are thought to be faulty in their methodology and of poor quality. A meta-analysis of these studies failed to confirm the efficacy or any proven benefit from this practice.333 Radical peritoneal d\'e9bridement of all fibrinous deposits on peritoneal surfaces is, likewise, no longer thought to be effective.350\
\
In extremely severe cases, a staged operative approach may be indicated in order to diminish the development of multiple organ system dysfunction related to infection induced immune system dysregulation. At times, a relaparotomy operation, either planned or on-demand may be necessary to achieve optimal d\'e9bridement of diseased and contaminated tissues. However, deciding on whether this is needed and, if so, when best to perform the procedure is not clearly defined but should be individualized based on patient specific parameters. Repeated entry into the inflamed peritoneum may escalate the cascade further. A review concluded that in the absence of randomized, controlled prospective trials with appropriate stratification of patients by severity of illness, evidence is now insufficient to determine whether these procedures improve outcome in severe diffuse peritonitis.351,352 Nevertheless, although damage control may be of utility, necessary, and indicated in some patients,353,354 neither planned nor mandatory repeat laparotomy should be applied to all patients with severe, diffuse peritonitis.4\
\
Less invasive source-control procedures, including ultrasound and CT-guided radiologic interventional percutaneous drainage of intraabdominal and pelvic fluid collections in selected patients, are increasingly relied on when the infection is localized and a safe window for introduction of the drainage tube exists.355\'96357 This treatment approach may also be the safest alternative in patients who could not tolerate a surgical procedure owing to their medical condition or who refused operation.\
\
Use of multiple drains for drainage of the general peritoneal cavity is physically impossible because exudate and adhesions rapidly isolate and occlude the drains and may increase secondary infections. Drains are often placed, however, in a dependent point to which fluid can be expected to gravitate or in an area of devitalized tissue that cannot be removed.\
\
In general, establishing adequate source control aimed at optimizing clinical outcome should be performed using the least invasive procedure that will satisfy the goal. However, such less invasive tactics may not be adequate in those patients with diffuse and severe peritonitis.342\
\
Prevention\
To prevent postoperative peritonitis, the peritoneum must not be contaminated with GI or vaginal secretions. In addition to the use of good surgical technique, contamination can be avoided with early treatment of an intraabdominal infection. Leigh and coworkers358 noted that the rate of wound infection in patients with perforated appendix was greater than 50% if no antimicrobial therapy was used but only 15% with appropriate therapy. Similarly, early use of antibiotics in penetrating wounds of the abdomen, especially wounds involving the colon, has been efficacious.359 Surgeons also have used various means to reduce the complex GI or vaginal flora before performing clean, contaminated surgery. Mechanical cleansing of the bowel with a low-residue diet followed by a liquid diet, cathartics, and enemas can reduce the total fecal mass and coliform count in the colon, although not the predominant anaerobic microbiota. The use of oral antibiotics preoperatively to reduce bowel flora is well accepted. E. coli in the colonic flora is sensitive to oral neomycin or kanamycin, whereas B. fragilis is frequently sensitive to erythromycin or metronidazole. Combinations such as neomycin and erythromycin base have been shown to be effective in reducing total bowel flora preoperatively and in decreasing the incidence of postoperative infection.360Page 969\
\
Parenteral antibiotics have also been used in GI and vaginal surgery prophylactically when there is a chance of contamination with normal microflora at the operative site (clean, contaminated surgery). Of these types of operations, 30% may be complicated by infections. Several studies have shown significant reduction in the frequency of postoperative infection, from as high as 30% to as low as 4% after prophylactic antibiotic use in clean, contaminated surgery.317,361,362 The basic principle of antibiotic prophylaxis is to provide adequate tissue levels at the site of contamination and adequate blood levels during the procedure given as one preoperative dose with redosing indicated in cases of prolonged operations. See Chapter 317 for a more detailed discussion.\
\
Peritoneal Dialysis\'96Associated Peritonitis\
Long-Term Peritoneal Dialysis\
\
PD has been used successfully to treat uremia in patients with end-stage renal disease since the mid-1940s. Peritonitis was a frequently associated side effect that hindered the acceptance of chronic PD until an improved access catheter was developed by Henry Tenckhoff in 1968. This catheter significantly decreased the incidence of peritonitis, but initial reports of patients undergoing continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) with this catheter indicated peritonitis rates of more than six episodes per patient-year.363 This rate has appeared to decline with the introduction of collapsible plastic bags, improved adapters (Y-system), and better techniques.364 However, peritonitis remains the major complication of CAPD today and is the chief reason for peritoneal catheter loss, discontinuation of PD, and switch to hemodialysis.365,366 The reported incidence rates of peritonitis vary widely by country and individual facility characterisitics.369 Historically, it has occurred at a first-year rate of 42 per patient-years, or one episode per patient-year (range, <0.5 to \uc0\u8805 3) of which, approximately 73% occurred during their initial 6 months of CAPD treatment.367,368 Currently, PD-associated peritonitis occurs in approximately 30%\'9640% of patients during their first course of PD treatment,367,369 with some centers now routinely report infection rates of less than one episode in 24 patient-months and as low as one episode in 60 patient-months.370 Peritonitis recurs in 20%\'9630% of patients. A small proportion of patients seem to have an unusually high frequency of peritonitis.\
\
This disparity has been attributed partly to faulty sterile technique on the part of patients during self-administration of CAPD (touch contamination). The patient undergoing CAPD needs to perform a great number of sterile exchanges per year (4 per day \'d7 365 days = 1460 per year); a patient on continuous cycling peritoneal dialysis (CCPD), in comparison, performs fewer exchanges. With CCPD, the rate of peritonitis would be presumably lower because manipulation of the connections is required less frequently; this concept is supported by most but not all studies.371,372\
\
Source of Infection and Risk Factors\
The origin of infection in most cases seems to be touch contamination of the catheter by common skin organisms.363,373 Alterations of skin flora in CAPD recipients374 may lead to peritoneal contamination with enteric pathogens. A higher incidence of peritonitis seems to occur in patients undergoing dialysis who are carriers of nasal S. aureus. This carriage state increases the likelihood of developing exit site infections and tunnel infections in comparison with noncarriers, but the overall peritonitis rate of the two groups was not different in one study, although all cases of S. aureus peritonitis occurred in the carriers.375 Pathogens may also contaminate the peritoneum from exit site infections and subcutaneous tunnel (periluminal) infections, transient bacteremia, and contamination of the dialysate delivery system during bag exchanges. As mentioned previously, enteric bacteria may also gain access to the peritoneal cavity by means of transmural migration through an intact intestinal wall after the introduction of hypertonic solutions into the peritoneum. This mechanism may account for enteric bacterial peritonitis in patients undergoing dialysis. On rare occasions, a vaginal leak may serve as a source of peritonitis. Endoscopic GI (colonoscopy, sigmoidoscopy), gynecologic (hysteroscopy), urologic (cystoscopy) procedures have been associated with a greater risk for the development of peritonitis in CAPD patients when a biopsy, polyp removal, or placement of an intrauterine device was performed.364,376 Dental procedures with resultant transient bacteremia also pose a risk of subsequent PD peritonitis. The risk of peritonitis is also higher in patients on CAPD who are chronically constipated, obese, or smoke; have chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; own domestic pets, have hypokalemia or hypoalbuminemia; or have depression.377\'96380 Polymicrobial infection with fecal organisms is suggestive of bowel perforation as a complication of catheter placement or secondary peritonitis from other causes such as cholecystitis, appendicitis, ruptured diverticulum, bowel ischemia, incarcerated hernia, or inadvertent perforation as the result of an endoscopic procedure.364\
\
Alterations in peritoneal defenses may increase the risk of peritonitis in patients undergoing CAPD. The antimicrobial function of peritoneal macrophages and PMN cells generally requires the presence of opsonins. A reduction in the levels of immunoglobulin G and C3 has been noted in PD effluents in comparison with serum, and the concentrations of these crucial opsonizing agents are related inversely to the frequency of peritonitis.381 Alterations in the protective mesothelial barrier of the peritoneum as well as dilution of resident cytokines and macrophages occur in the presence of the large quantity of fluid introduced with PD.382 Other important factors that impair host defense mechanisms are the low pH, increased osmolality, glucose concentration, and glucose degradation products in PD fluid, all of which can impair PMN leukocyte function and antibiotic efficacy.383 Newer PD fluids containing glucose polymers (e.g., icodextrin) may be less detrimental to macrophage and PMN leukocyte activity. However, the use of more biocompatible dialysis fluids has not conclusively been found to decrease the risk of peritonitis.384,385 The formation of biofilm on the catheter is often found, but its presence has not been clearly associated with the development of peritonitis.386\'96388 However, it does appear to contribute to relapsing or recurrent infection with the same organism and to decreased therapeutic responses and development of antimicrobial resistance.389\
\
Microbiology\
Bacteria account for the preponderance of PD-related infections. Gram-positive organisms historically have constituted 60%\'9680% of isolates, most commonly Staphylococcus epidermidis and Streptococcus spp., followed by S. aureus, Enterococcus spp., and Corynebacterium spp. More recent surveys have found a decrease in single gram-positive organism cases (45%\'9665%) and a concomitant increase in other, less previously involved pathogens.390 Staphylococcal isolates have been noted to grow on polymer surfaces and frequently produce an extracellular slime substance or biofilm (as just described) that may protect these bacteria from host defenses.389 VRE as nosocomial pathogens are now more frequently recognized in peritonitis cases and when encountered should raise concern for another possible intraabdominal process.391 Gram-negative organisms are obtained from 15%\'9635% of isolates. E. coli is the most common, followed by Klebsiella and Enterobacter spp., Proteus spp., and Pseudomonas spp. Cases caused by gram-negative organisms have increased, according to a retrospective study of PD-related peritonitis over a decade-long period.392,393 Sources may include contaminated water, skin, urinary tract, bowel, and animal contacts (e.g., Pasteurella multocida). Less common pathogens include Acinetobacter spp. and anaerobic bacteria. Rare isolates include atypical mycobacteria (usually Mycobacterium chelonae or Mycobacterium fortuitum), M. tuberculosis, and Nocardia asteroides complex. Fungi are found to be the etiologic pathogen in 2%\'9613% of PD peritonitis cases, and most times involve C. albicans and Candida parapsilosis.394 Molds (e.g., Aspergillus, Fusarium, and Mucorales) and non-Candida yeasts (Rhodotorula) are very infrequently implicated in PD peritonitis.395 Although it is unclear whether PD-related peritonitis can be caused by viruses, the risk of bacterial infection is thought to increase in patients with a viral infection.\
\
Polymicrobial peritonitis in patients undergoing PD is rare (1%\'964%) and is usually assumed to be secondary to a primary intestinal process (e.g., bowel perforation, ischemia) and usually necessitates surgical exploration.364,393\
\
Diagnosis\
The updated 2022 International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis (ISPD) peritonitis guidelines for the diagnosis of PD peritonitis are listed in Table 76.7.Page 970\
\
\'a0\
TABLE 76.7\
\
2022 ISPD Guidelines Used to Diagnose Peritoneal Dialysis Peritonitis\
1. Clinical features consistent with peritonitis, that is, abdominal pain and/or cloudy dialysis effluent\
2. Dialysis effluent white cell count >100/\uc0\u956 L or >0.1 \'d7 109/L (after a dwell time of at least 2 h), with >50% polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMNs)\
3. Positive dialysis effluent culture\
Diagnosis of peritonitis is made when microorganisms and an increased number of leukocytes are present in the dialysate in combination with a constellation of clinical findings that include abdominal pain and tenderness (60%\'9680% of patients), nausea and vomiting (30%\'9650%), fever (25%\'9650%), and diarrhea (10%).363,396 Not all these criteria need to be met, however, for the diagnosis to be made.\
\
The dialysate is almost always cloudy, and microscopic examination reveals a leukocyte count greater than 100 cells/mm3, which provides a sensitivity of 80% and a specificity of 89%, with polymorphonuclear cell (neutrophils) predominance (>50% of WBC) increasing specificity to 97% for PD-associated peritonitis.397 The length of the dwell time has an impact on the number of effluent cells and is required to be at least 2 hours. Cell counts obtained from fluid that has dwelled in the abdomen for more than 15 hours may be greater than 100 cells/mm3 but are macrophage predominant and do not necessarily indicate the presence of peritontis.398 A low leukocyte count in dialysate may also be due to a defective host immune response or could be indicative of a tunnel infection and not peritonitis. Although a predominance of lymphocytes may be encountered with fungal and mycobacterial infections, the majority of cases still manifest a larger number of neutrophils in the PD effluent. A preponderance of eosinophils in the peritoneal fluid can similarly be seen when the causative agent is a typically involved bacteria.399 A self-limited condition called eosinophilic peritonitis often follows placement of the Tenckhoff catheter and may represent allergy to the tubing or the laparoscopic instillation of carbon dioxide. Peritoneal eosinophilia may also be present in fungal and parasitic peritonitis, may be related to allergic reactions or chemical and drug (i.e., vancomycin) effects and may be associated with icodextrin dialysate.400 Gram staining of the fluid reveals organisms in less than 10% of cases.364,401 The diagnostic yield of the Gram stain is increased if it is performed on a centrifuged specimen. Gram stains have been found to be helpful in early detection of fungal elements, facilitating timely diagnosis and management of fungal peritonitis.402 Peripheral leukocytosis is a poor indicator for peritonitis in this group of patients. Blood cultures are rarely positive in patients with PD peritonitis when signs of sepsis are absent,403 in contrast to the 30%\'9650% positive rate in other types of intraabdominal infections.\
\
A pathogen is successfully isolated from peritoneal fluid in approximately 72%\'9686% of PD peritonitis cases when the fluid has been collected appropriately.369 Constant flow of dialysis fluid into and out of the peritoneal cavity dilutes the microbial density and may falsely lower the rate of positive results of dialysate culture. Negative cultures also may result from infection with fastidious organisms, from previous antimicrobial treatment, or from inadequate culture techniques (e.g., the collection of too little effluent). Culturing the sediment after centrifuging 50 mL of effluent dialysate or placing 5\'9610 mL of effluent dialysate in each of two blood culture bottles will enhance the recovery rate of organisms.364,404 All cultures should be performed aerobically. Fungal, mycobacterial, and anaerobic cultures should be performed if clinically indicated (e.g., negative aerobic cultures). If cultures remain negative after 3\'965 days of incubation, PD effluent should be sent for repeat cell count, differential count, and fungal and mycobacterial culture. In addition, subculture on selective media for 3\'964 additional days may aid in identification of slow-growing fastidious bacteria and yeasts that are undetectable in some automated culture systems.364 PD catheter cultures have been found to increase pathogen detection.405 Causes of turbid dialysate, such as hemorrhage, fibrin or other proteins, chylous ascites, lymphoma and other malignancies, and prolonged dwell time, should be considered if the leukocyte count is below 300\'96500 cells/mm3.406,407\
\
Newer diagnostic techniques that have been proposed for the early diagnosis of peritonitis, include leukocyte esterase reagent strips, biomarker assays (procalcitonin), PCR assays, and 16 S rRNA gene sequencing. To date, none have proven to be more sensitive than currently employed diagnostic techniques.408,409 Radiologic imaging studies are neither specific nor particularly helpful in the diagnosis of PD-associated peritonitis. Small amounts of free intraperitoneal air can, at times, be discovered in asymptomatic patients secondary to entry into the peritoneal cavity during PD exchanges.\
\
Treatment and Prognosis\
The prognosis of peritonitis in dialysis recipients is generally favorable. PD peritonitis\'96associated mortality has been reported to range from 2%\'965%; however, in one retrospective study, death occurred in 6% of 565 patients with a total of 693 episodes of peritonitis.410,411 Mortality rates have also been found to vary according to the specific etiologic pathogen, the highest with fungal pathogens, gram-negative bacilli, and S. aureus.411,412 The duration of illness and positive peritoneal fluid cultures after institution of antimicrobial therapy is usually 1\'964 days. Some infections, especially infections caused by S. aureus, Pseudomonas spp., or fungus, resolve more slowly, however, and may cause relapse more frequently.\
\
Adequate levels of antimicrobial agents necessary to treat peritonitis successfully can be obtained in the peritoneal fluid through either the systemic or intraperitoneal route. Because CAPD-associated peritonitis is a localized infection, however, the intraperitoneal route is preferred and in fact has been found to be superior to the intravenous route.413 Nevertheless, parenteral antibiotics should be administered in septic or bacteremic patients. The increased use of intraperitoneal antibiotic therapy for peritonitis has allowed most patients to be treated on an ambulatory basis. Hospitalization is indicated for patients who are severely ill or who are unable to manage the administration of intraperitoneal antibiotics at home. Although a variety of dosages and drugs can be found in the literature, the initial dosages recommended in Table 76.8 for intraperitoneal administration result in effective peritoneal fluid drug concentrations. Subsequent dosing is used to maintain these levels. The aim of the dosing regimen is to maintain a concentration of the drug in the peritoneal cavity fluid greater than the MIC of the offending pathogen for most, if not all, of the dosing interval. However, intermittent dosing regimens (antimicrobials given once daily) and continuous dosing regimens (given in each exchange) have been found to produce largely equivalent results.413 With intermittent dosing, the antimicrobial must dwell for at least 6 hours. Physicians must exercise caution when reviewing the MIC and minimal bactericidal concentration data because these concentrations have been markedly increased when PD effluent is used as the in\'a0vitro growth medium.414\
\
Because of the lack of comparative, prospective clinical trials, no antimicrobial regimen is superior to another.415 After cultures are obtained, initial empirical antimicrobial therapy should be based on the results of Gram staining or, if the Gram stain is not helpful, directed against the most likely pathogens, providing coverage against both gram-positive and gram-negative organisms. The ISPD 2022 guidelines recommend that for the coverage of gram-positive organisms, vancomycin or first-generation cephalosporin be used. However, vancomycin should be considered in centers with a high prevalence of methicillin-resistant organisms. The threshold prevalence of methicillin resistance that justifies empirical use of vancomycin remains controversial. For the gram-negative coverage, third-generation cephalosporin or aminoglycoside is suggested keeping in mind that treatment failure with cephalosporins is high with rising prevalence of ESBL-producing organisms. Monotherapy for empirical treatment of peritonitis, instead of combination therapy, has now been accepted as an effective strategy. Cefepime monotherapy was shown to be effective and noninferior to standard dual therapy with cefazolin plus ceftazidime.416 Aminoglycosides remain useful for treating gram-negative peritonitis. Because aminoglycosides exhibit concentration-dependent activity, their maximal bacterial killing occurs at high peak drug concentrations. Furthermore, aminoglycosides continue to suppress bacterial growth even after drug concentration falls below MIC of the bacteria, as a result of their prolonged postantibiotic effect. Based on these PD characteristics and to minimize toxicity and adaptive resistance while maintaining drug efficacy, intermittent daily Page 971dosing of intraperitoneal aminoglycosides is recommended. A significant concern with aminoglycoside use in patients on PD is ototoxicity, both vestibular and cochlear. At the currently recommended peritonitis treatment dosage of aminoglycosides, ototoxicity could occur in patients on PD, resulting in either vestibular or cochlear damage similar to that which occurs with systemic administration.417 Controlled trials of N-acetylcysteine, aimed at providing antioxidant protection of aminoglycoside-induced cochlear ototoxicity, have yielded positive results, though none of these randomized controlled trials assessed vestibular ototoxiciy.418,419 Consequently, the coadministration of N-acetylcysteine at 600 mg twice daily for patients on PD requiring aminoglycoside seems practical. However, in the absence of high-quality evidence to ameliorate the potentially irreversible risk of aminoglycoside ototoxicity, the best measure is to curtail prolonged or repeated administration when an alternative and safer antimicrobial agent is available. The fluoroquinolones antibiotics ciprofloxacin and moxifloxacin are compatible with PD solutions and shown to be highly active and bactericidal in PD fluids with concentration-dependent activity. Because both ciprofloxacin and moxifloxacin can achieve adequate levels within the peritoneum when given by mouth, oral administration is an alternative and more convenient choice if the organism has been proven to be susceptible to these agents.420\'96422 However, owing to the increase in fluoroquinolone-resistant enteric bacteria, they should be avoided when known local fluoroquinolone resistance rates are greater than 10%.420 Initial antibiotic choices should be modified, if necessary, after culture results are obtained. P. aeruginosa peritonitis is associated with high rates of treatment failure and relapses423 even though the antibiotic resistance rate of Pseudomonas spp. causing peritonitis has been stable over the years. The presence of other virulence factors such as biofilm production has been postulated as a possible cause. The 2022 ISPD guidelines recommend for the treatment of P. aeruginosa PD peritonitis that, based on susceptibility results, two effective antibiotics with different mechanisms of antimicrobial activity be used, because retrospective case series showed  that the use of two  antipseudomonal antibiotics is associated with better outcomes.423,424 In addition to antibiotics, Page 972catheter removal may be needed when Pseudomonas is the infecting pathogen. If vancomycin-resistant enterococci are determined to be the causative microorganism, linezolid or daptomycin should be administered.425 The minimal length of therapy needed for dialysis-related peritonitis has not been sufficiently determined in high-quality randomized studies, but the usual duration ranges from 14\'9621 days based on the organism involved.364 Except for coagulase-negative Staphylococcus, Streptococcus spp., and Corynebacterium spp., where 2 weeks of therapy is adequate, longer antibiotic treatment courses (21 days) are required for peritonitis due to S. aureus, Enterococcus spp., P. aeruginosa, Acinetobacter spp., S. maltophilia, and Enterobacterales.\
\
Newer diagnostic techniques that have been proposed for the early diagnosis of peritonitis, include leukocyte esterase reagent strips, biomarker assays (procalcitonin), PCR assays, and 16 S rRNA gene sequencing. To date, none have proven to be more sensitive than currently employed diagnostic techniques.408,409 Radiologic imaging studies are neither specific nor particularly helpful in the diagnosis of PD-associated peritonitis. Small amounts of free intraperitoneal air can, at times, be discovered in asymptomatic patients secondary to entry into the peritoneal cavity during PD exchanges. \
\
Polymicrobial PD peritonitis is uncommon and when present suggests a concurrent intraabdominal pathologic process.425 CT imaging may help identify the pathologic condition, and surgical consultation should be obtained. When a surgical cause of peritonitis is suspected, coverage for anaerobes and gram-negative enteric bacilli is required. The antibiotics of choice are metronidazole plus vancomycin, in combination with ceftazidime or an aminoglycoside. If laparotomy is needed, the PD catheter is required to be removed and antibiotics are continued intravenously. For culture-negative peritonitis episodes that improve promptly with antibiotics, they are probably caused by gram-positive organisms and initial therapy should be continued. Duration of therapy should be limited to 2 weeks because treatment outcomes were similar between episodes with treatment durations of 2 weeks and 3 weeks.426\
\
Most patients with CAPD-associated peritonitis exhibit clinical improvement within 48\'9696 hours after initiation of antimicrobial therapy and the fluid should appear less cloudy. Refractory peritonitis is defined as failure of the PD effluent to clear with persistently cloudy bags or persistent dialysis effluent leukocyte count greater than 100 cells/mm3 after 5 days of appropriate antibiotic therapy.364 If the signs and symptoms of peritonitis persist after 5 days of treatment of therapy, this suggests a lack of response to treatment427 and reevaluation is warranted; the possibilities of resistant pathogens, unusual organisms (e.g., mycobacterial, fungal), and other intraabdominal processes should be considered. Consideration of changing from intermittent to continuous dosing should also be considered.\
\
Fungal peritonitis, usually caused by C. albicans and C. parapsilosis, historically has been treated with amphotericin B,428,429 although the antifungal treatment of choice for C. albicans is usually fluconazole. Other Candida organisms sometimes require an echinocandin (caspofungin, micafungin, or anidulafungin) or voriconazole.430,431 Echinocandins are currently the preferred initial antifungal agent,232 particularly in patients with prior exposure to azole antifungal agents and need to be administered intravenously due to the diminished activity of echinocandins against Candida spp. biofilm in the presence of PD fluid. Patients should be transitioned to fluconazole when clinically stable and susceptibility to fluconazole has been confirmed. The preferred route of administration for both fluconazole and voriconazole is oral.379 If a mold is isolated, initial therapy with amphotericin B (deoxycholate or lipid formulations) is warranted, although for Aspergillus spp., oral voriconazole, posaconazole or, isavuconazole given orally are efficacious and reasonable alternatives.432 Some molds, including Fusarium spp., may be resistant to amphotericin, however. Flucytosine is not recommended in azotemic patients because of potentially lethal toxicity to the colon and bone marrow. Ketoconazole is no longer used. If CAPD is continued, amphotericin B should be given intraperitoneally, but it can cause appreciable abdominal pain when given by this route. There is also concern that amphotericin B\'96induced inflammation may cause adhesions that reduce the effective dialyzing surface. Notwithstanding the availability of newer antifungal drugs, catheter removal remains the cornerstone of managing fungal peritonitis and is required in all patients with CAPD-associated fungal infections.364 Failure to do so has been found to result in mortality of 50%\'9691%, which is 2\'963 times higher than in those in whom the catheter is promptly removed.433 The odds of resuming PD are also significantly greater in those who have undergone early catheter removal.434 The duration of antifungal treatment has not been sufficiently determined. Most experts recommend 4 weeks of treatment after the catheter has been removed and until all signs and symptoms of infection have resolved. During this time patients are converted to hemodialysis and reinsertion of the catheter and resumption of PD can take place after at least 2 weeks of antifungal treatment, although in the majority of reports the median period of time was 15 weeks.435\
\
\
Nontuberculous mycobacterium PD peritonitis is uncommon and should be considered in patients on PD with peritonitis that are culture-negative or refractory to empirical antibiotic therapy.436 Quite often there is a delay in diagnosis, which may be improved by requesting examination for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) using Ziehl-Neelsen staining and specific mycobacterial culture media in all culture-negative cases particularly when Gram stain of the fluid suggests diphtheroids or Corynebacterium spp., for which these organisms may be mistaken. M. fortuitum and M. chelonae account for the majority of NTM peritonitis episodes.437 Few data exist as to the optimal treatment of these AFB pathogens. Antibiotic therapy should be guided by the isolated species, known susceptibility patterns, and then in\'a0vitro antimicrobial sensitivities. Generally, two agents to which the isolate is susceptible for a minimum of 6 weeks together with removal of the PD catheter is the recommended treatment strategy.438 In less than one-fifth of cases have patients been able to resume PD.439\
\
Additional nonantimicrobial interventions such as routine peritoneal lavage, the use of fibrinolytic agents, and the instillation of intraperitoneal immunoglobulins have not proved beneficial and therefore serve no role in the management of PD-associated peritonitis.413 Nonetheless, the addition of heparin 500 units/L intraperitoneally to prevent occlusion of the catheter by fibrin in patients with cloudy effluent may be beneficial. Rapid-cycle peritoneal lavage on day 1 or from day 3 to 5 has not been found to improve the rate of cure or relapse.440 The temporary use of icodextrin solution during acute peritonitis has been reported to be associated with a higher cure rate.441 In most instances there is no benefit to stopping PD when peritonitis is diagnosed, and its discontinuation may lead to complications such as injury to the peritoneal surface.442\
\
Catheter Removal and Prevention\
Removal of the catheter is necessary in 10%\'9620% of patients (Table 76.9). The indications for catheter removal include persistent infection at the skin exit site or tunnel; fungal, fecal, or mycobacterial peritonitis; persistent peritonitis despite 5 days of treatment; recurrent or relapsing peritonitis with the same organism; and catheter malfunction (e.g., poor flow). Quite often to cure P. aeruginosa peritonitis the catheter must also be removed. The catheter should also be removed in patients with intraperitoneal abscess. In the setting of relapsing peritonitis, a new catheter can be placed at the time of removal if the fluid is clear, but not when removed for recalcitrant or difficult-to-sterilize cases such as fungal infection. Use of oral or intraperitoneal antibiotics has not been shown to be effective in preventing peritonitis during PD. However, systemic prophylactic antibiotics need to be administered immediately before catheter placement using a first-generation cephalosporin. Even though use of systemic antibiotic prophylaxis is successful for catheter-related peritonitis prevention, it is unclear if it prevents exit site infections.443 In addition, topical mupirocin applied to the exit site or intranasal application of mupirocin has been used to  eliminate nasal carriage with S. aureus and has been shown to reduce exit site infections, but has not yet been shown to significantly reduce the incidence of CAPD-related peritonitis.444 Patients need to seek advice immediately from the treatment team if contamination during PD exchange is noted, and prophylactic antibiotics after wet contamination of the PD system to prevent peritonitis are strongly recommended.364Page 973\
\
Antibiotic prophylaxis is indicated before colonoscopy and invasive gynecologic procedures. For colonoscopy intravenous antibiotic prophylaxis, potential choices include cephalosporins (such as ceftriaxone or ceftazidime), amoxicillin-clavulanate, ampicillin-sulbactam, ampicillin plus aminoglycoside, with an aim to target most of the organisms described previously that cause peritonitis after colonoscopy. For gynecologic procedures, reasonable regimens should cover gram-positive and gram-negative bacterial isolates from the upper tract of female reproductive tracts. Examples include intravenous cefazolin or ceftriaxone before the procedure or oral cefadroxil 500 mg once daily for 3 days.364 Drainage of PD fluid to keep the abdomen empty before endoscopic GI and invasive or instrumental gynecologic procedures to keep the abdomen dry is also recommended. The abdomen is then kept dry for the next 24 hours. Amoxicillin 2 g PO 2 hours before planned dental procedures is also recommended. To prevent fungal peritonitis, the ISPD 2022 guidelines recommend that antifungal prophylaxis be coprescribed whenever patients on PD receive an antibiotic course, regardless of the indication for that antibiotic course. Direct contact with animals has been associated with the development of PD peritonitis with several reports of P. multocida as well as Capnocytophaga canimorsus being linked to a patient\'92s exposure to cats.445,446 Patients need to take extra precautions to prevent peritonitis if domestic pets are kept, and it is suggested that pets not be allowed in the room where PD exchange takes place, and where dialysis tubing, equipment, and machines are stored.\
\
Acute Peritoneal Dialysis\
The incidence of peritonitis during acute PD has remained stable since the 1980s. Innovations in technique, which began during the 1960s, reduced the rate of peritonitis from 50% to lower levels. These innovations included closed drainage systems, small-bore catheters, limitation of dialysis to no longer than 72 hours, incorporation of a Millipore filter into the tubing, and development of closed automatic systems. Use of dry-heat incubators to warm the dialysate also decreases the risk of contamination that may occur when water baths are used for this purpose.\
\
Antibiotic-resistant, hospital-acquired, gram-negative bacilli and staphylococci frequently cause peritonitis during acute PD. It is recommended that therapy be initiated with intraperitoneal vancomycin and gentamicin (or tobramycin), with or without concurrent or subsequent parenteral infusion of the same antibiotics, depending on the severity of the illness and the response to initial therapy (see Table 76.8 for dosages). The antibiotic regimen should be modified when the culture results become available. The clinical manifestations, prognosis, and response to therapy are like those described previously for peritonitis associated with chronic PD.\
